/**
\page plugin How to create a plugin
\section plugin_cmake Including the CMake file
\subsection plugin_cmake_win Windows
In your yoyo installation directory, there is a lib directory. Add this directory to the to the
CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH provided to CMake when configuring.

\subsection plugin_cmake_linux Linuxoid Systems
Installing the Yoyo sdk package will install the CMake module to your /usr/lib/cmake/ directory,
where CMake will automatically search for package descriptions.

\subsection plugin_cmake_definition Use Yoyo Sdk
Using
\code{.cmake}
find_package(yoyo)
\endcode
should get you the target definition.
\code{.cmake}
target_link_libraries(my_target Yoyo::Sdk)
\endcode
This will add the Yoyo include directories and link-libraries to your library.

\subsection plugin_cmake_utilities Some CMake utilities
If you are working on a Windows System, you might find this package interesting:
\code{.cmake}
include(GenerateExportHeader)
generate_export_header(my-plugin EXPORT_MACRO_NAME MY_PLUGIN_EXPORT)
\endcode
See https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/module/GenerateExportHeader.html for more information.

\section plugin_node Node creation
To find a description how to define new node types please consult the \ref nodes page.

\section plugin_documentation Node documentation
When installing a node to a yoyo::node_factory, you will notice, that a yoyo::documentation object
is necessary here as well.

This yoyo::documentation object will be used by the Property Widget to display a title for the
property if the camelCased Qt property name is not descriptive enough and it will use the toolTip
attribute of the property to provide some help to the user.

It will also be used by the persistance engine to omit storing default values.

Taking this, it makes sense to provide all possible bits of information into the yoyo::documentation
object.

You do not need to define the property documentation for the Yoyo base classes you used. There are
convenience functions adding documentation on the properties of these classes.

\section plugin_json Plugin descriptions
\subsection plugin_json_qt Qt plugin description
When creating a plugin with Qt you need a json file. This file is named at declaration and adds some
meta information to the plugin binary. This file is mandatory, but it can be empty.
\subsection plugin_json_yoyo Yoyo plugin description
The Yoyo plugin management provides the possibility to load plugins from any location, not just the
plugin directory inside the installation folder. For this and for the user to be able to distinguish
different plugins, each plugin needs a json file containing the plugin description. This json file
has to look like this:
\code{.json}
{
  "id": "2730bf79-0630-4a0b-8011-27d6dea0c363",
  "name": "test plugin",
  "description": "This is a plugin for testing purposes.",
  "path": "test-plugin.dll",
  "dependencies": []
}
\endcode
The items "name" and "description" will be shown in the Plugin Manager to help the user manage the
individual plugins. The "id" is used as internal identifier. Please make sure it is unique for your
plugin (https://www.uuidgenerator.net/).

In "path" you specify the path to your plugin binary. This can be an absolute path or a path
relative to the location of the description file.

With "dependencies" you can list a number of plugins (their ids) which need to be loaded before this
plugin can be loaded.

\section plugin_plugin Create the Plugin class
To creat a Yoyo plugin, you have to create a class derived from yoyo::yoyo_plugin, implementing all
purely virtual methods.
\code{.cpp}
#pragma once
#include "test-plugin_export.h"

#include "yoyo/yoyo_plugin.h"

class YOYO_TEST_PLUGIN_EXPORT test_plugin: public yoyo_plugin
{
    Q_OBJECT
    Q_PLUGIN_METADATA(IID "org.yoyo.plugin.Interface.test" FILE "test_plugin.json")
    Q_INTERFACES(yoyo::yoyo_plugin)
public:
    auto name() const->QString override;
    auto install_gui_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& gui_factory)->void override;
    auto install_data_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& data_factory)->void override;
    auto install_communication_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& communication_factory)->void override;
    auto install_protocol_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& protocol_factory)->void override;
    auto uninstall_gui_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& gui_factory)->void override;
    auto uninstall_data_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& data_factory)->void override;
    auto uninstall_communication_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& communication_factory)->void override;
    auto uninstall_protocol_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& protocol_factory)->void override;
};
\endcode
\note On Windows, you need an export macro; this is not necessary for Linuxoid systems

Using the Q_PLUGIN_METADATA macro you tell Qt which id it should use internally and you specify the
name of the previously created json (\ref plugin_json_qt).

With the Q_INTERFACES you declare which interfaces you are implementing.

\warning Per shared/dynamic library only one of these classes is allowed.

This could be a possible implementation for a plugin defining a leaf node for each category.
\code{.cpp}
#include "test_plugin.hpp"

#include "my_data_node.h"
#include "my_gui_node.h"
#include "my_protocol_node.h"
#include "my_communication_node.h"

#include "yoyo/node_factory.h"

auto test_plugin::name() const->QString { return "test-plugin"; }

auto test_plugin::install_gui_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& gui_factory)->void
{
    gui_factory.install_node(
        my_gui_node::typeId(),
        "my_gui_node",
        &my_gui_node::staticMetaObject,
        create_my_gui_documentation(),
        [](auto id) {
            return std::make_shared<my_gui_node>(id);
        },
        [](auto) {
            return yoyo::node_factory::node_id_list{};
        });
}

auto test_plugin::install_data_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& data_factory)->void
{
    data_factory.install_node(
        my_data_node::typeId(),
        "my_data_node",
        &my_data_node::staticMetaObject,
        create_my_data_documentation(),
        [](auto id) {
            return std::make_shared<my_data_node>(id);
        },
        [](auto) {
            return yoyo::node_factory::node_id_list{};
        });
}

auto test_plugin::install_communication_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& communication_factory)->void
{
    communication_factory.install_node(
        my_communication_node::typeId(),
        "my_communication_node",
        &my_communication_node::staticMetaObject,
        create_my_communication_documentation(),
        [](auto id) {
            return std::make_shared<my_communication_node>(id);
        },
        [](auto) {
            return yoyo::node_factory::node_id_list{};
        });
}

auto test_plugin::install_protocol_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& protocol_factory)->void
{
    protocol_factory.install_node(
        my_protocol_node::typeId(),
        "my_protocol_node",
        &my_protocol_node::staticMetaObject,
        create_my_protocol_documentation(),
        [](auto id) {
            return std::make_shared<my_protocol_node>(id);
        },
        [](auto) {
            return yoyo::node_factory::node_id_list{};
        });
}

auto test_plugin::uninstall_gui_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& gui_factory)->void { gui_factory.uninstall_node(id_gui); }
auto test_plugin::uninstall_data_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& data_factory)->void { data_factory.uninstall_node(id_data); }
auto test_plugin::uninstall_communication_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& communication_factory)->void { communication_factory.uninstall_node(id_communication); }
auto test_plugin::uninstall_protocol_nodes(yoyo::node_factory& protocol_factory)->void { protocol_factory.uninstall_node(id_protocol); }
\endcode
*/
